content.text:
The assertion fires because `rb_insert` is still an unimplemented stub in [src/rbtree.c:59-69](src/rbtree.c#L59-L69) — it unconditionally does `return -1;` regardless of its arguments. The very first case in `insert_cases` ([test_rbtree.c:377](tests/test_rbtree.c#L377)) is `insert_into_empty_success` with `expect_ok = 1`, so `rc == 0` is false while `c->expect_ok` is true, and `assert((rc == 0) == c->expect_ok)` trips. This is expected given only `rb_create` has been implemented so far (per the commit history) — `rb_find`, `rb_foreach`, and `rb_validate` are stubs too.

One thing worth flagging: `main()` at the bottom of the file now calls the test functions in a different order than before —

```c
int main(void)
{
    test_rb_validate();
    test_rb_insert();
    test_rb_create();
    return 0;
}
```

I didn't make this change; it must have been edited directly. It's why you're seeing the failure land in `test_rb_insert` rather than `test_rb_create`: `test_rb_validate` now runs first, and it happens to pass *vacuously* — every one of its fixtures asserts `rb_validate(t) != 0`, which the stub trivially satisfies by always returning `-1`, whether or not the real invariant-checking logic exists yet. That's not a real pass, just a coincidence of the stub's return value, so I'd suggest putting `test_rb_create()` back first (it's the only function with real assertions that can currently succeed or fail meaningfully) — let me know if you want me to revert the order or if it was intentional.

This is a real, currently-expected leak — and it's coming from `rb_destroy`, not the tests. `rb_destroy` is still a stub ([src/rbtree.c:102-112](src/rbtree.c#L102-L112)):

```c
void rb_destroy(rbtree_t *t)
{
    if (!t)
        return;
    /* TODO: once rb_insert lands real nodes, traverse and free each node's
     * key (and value, iff value_free is set) before freeing nil/t. ... */
    rb_free(t->nil);
    rb_free(t);
}
```

It only frees `t->nil` and `t` — it never walks `t->root`. That's harmless for every tree built through `rb_insert` today, since `rb_insert` is also a stub that always fails and never actually allocates a node. But `test_rb_validate`'s fixtures build real, non-empty trees directly via the white-box `mk_node` helper (bypassing `rb_insert`), each allocating a small cluster of `rbnode_t` + key-copy pairs, then call `rb_destroy(t)` on that non-empty tree.

The numbers line up exactly: `test_rb_validate` has 11 fixture cases (4 red-red, 4 BST-order, 2 black-height, 1 root-color), so Valgrind reports 11 "definitely lost" blocks — one per fixture, the node in each small tree that nothing else points to — and 43 "indirectly lost" blocks, the rest of each fixture's nodes and key-copies, reachable only through that first lost node's `left`/`right`/`parent`/`key` pointers. 11 + 43 = 54 = 27 nodes × 2 allocations each (`rbnode_t` + key copy), matching every node built across all 11 fixtures.

So this isn't a test bug: it's the direct consequence of `rb_destroy` not yet traversing the tree, combined with `test_rb_validate` deliberately building real trees to exercise `rb_validate`'s fixtures. It'll clear once `rb_destroy` is implemented to walk `t->root` and free each node's key (and owned value) — already flagged by its own TODO comment. Not something to change in the tests.
